\code{run_plan()} gives it a new one with \code{\link[data.table:setalloccol]{data.table::setalloccol()}}. It does
this for each data.table in a value or in a plain list in a value, at any
depth. It does not do this for a data.table in a list column of a
data.frame. Such a value MAY differ in \code{identical()} between 1 worker and
more.

\code{run_plan()} runs every analysis, also after an analysis fails. An error in
an action function fails that analysis, and an error in a data function
fails each analysis that gets the data. This is the same with 1 worker and
with more. After the run, \code{run_plan()} prints each failed analysis with its
name and its full error message with \code{cat()}, and then it stops. R cuts an
error message at 1000 bytes by default, and \code{cat()} does not. The error
message of \code{run_plan()} also names the first failed analysis in the order
of the plan, and holds its error. pbn cuts that error to keep the message
below 900 bytes. When 2 or more analyses fail and all have the same error,
the message says so. pbn then saves no results, because \code{\link[=save_results]{save_results()}}
needs the value of \code{run_plan()}. The results files of an earlier run stay
as they are. After \code{\link[=initialize_project]{initialize_project()}}, \code{run_plan()} writes the run log
\verb{<version>_results/run.md} with the failures before it stops. The section
The run log of \code{\link[=save_results]{save_results()}} describes it.

After \code{\link[=initialize_project]{initialize_project()}}, \code{run_plan()} first replaces the run log with
one that says that the run has not finished, and deletes the errors file
\verb{<version>_results/run_errors_gitexclude.md}. A call of \code{run_plan()}
without \code{\link[=save_results]{save_results()}} after it leaves this log.

\code{run_plan()} measures the elapsed time of each data step and of each
analysis where it runs, also on a worker. The times go to the record of
the plan, and \code{\link[=save_results]{save_results()}} writes them to the run log. They are not
in the value, so two runs stay \code{identical()}.

The value carries the attribute \code{pbn_run}, a stamp of the plan. It holds
no count of the runs, so two runs of one plan can be \code{identical()}.
\code{\link[=save_results]{save_results()}} stops on a value without the stamp of its plan.

\code{run_plan()} sets \code{use_foreach} of the plan: \code{FALSE} for 1 worker and
\code{TRUE} for more.

After \code{\link[=initialize_project]{initialize_project()}}, \code{run_plan()} first computes the md5 of each
raw data file again when its size or its modification time changed. The
value carries the md5 in its stamp, and \code{\link[=save_results]{save_results()}} writes it to
\code{manifest$data_md5}.

An analysis that changes the shared data or an object of the functions
folder in place fails. An analysis that leaves a new object in the global
environment fails too. \code{\link[=add_analyses]{add_analyses()}} gives the rules.

An error outside the wrappers of pbn ends the run. Examples are a worker
that stops, and an error of plnr or of the backend of the workers.
\code{run_plan()} then prints the whole error, and writes the run log with it
after \code{\link[=initialize_project]{initialize_project()}}. It stops with one message that holds the
error, cut below 900 bytes, and saves nothing. The future plan, the
foreach backend and the options go back as after any run, and the
cluster stops.

For a data function with \code{cache = TRUE}, \code{run_plan()} needs
\code{\link[=initialize_project]{initialize_project()}} first, and it prints one line for each scenario and
simulation. The section Cache of \code{\link[=add_data]{add_data()}} gives the rules. A data
